From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Randolph <noreply@platform.uno>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: classify GC handles rooting condemned collectible ALCs

A downstream WASM host doing repeated collectible-ALC load/unload cycles
still strands one context per cycle with the forced-unload opt-in
active: the teardown reports NotQuiescent indefinitely, so some root
keeps the condemned contexts' LoaderAllocator witnesses alive. An
exhaustive managed-side investigation (a BFS root tracer over every
non-collectible static, ConditionalWeakTable enumeration, and direct
probes of the interop/RPC tables) found no managed root -- which leaves
exactly the roots managed reflection CANNOT enumerate: raw GCHandles,
thread stacks/registers, and native structures.

This patch adds read-only diagnostics that narrow that down from inside
the runtime, extending the existing InternalGetScoutRetirementStats
surface (unknown kinds keep returning -1, so the addition is invisible
to every existing consumer):

  * mono_gc_count_handles_into_managers (sgen-mono.c) walks the sgen
    handle tables with sgen_gchandle_iterate and, for every handle whose
    TARGET object's class belongs to one of the supplied memory
    managers, bumps a per-handle-type bucket. The walk is read-only by
    construction: the iteration callback always returns the slot value
    it was handed, so no handle is freed, moved or re-tagged.
  * assembly-load-context.c builds the condemned set (every collectible
    + unloading context's primary manager plus each shared generic
    manager it owns) and exposes:
      kind 11 -- strong (Normal) handles into the condemned set
      kind 12 -- pinned handles
      kind 13 -- weak (short) handles
      kind 14 -- weak-track-resurrection handles
      kind 15 -- condemned memory managers in scope (the denominator;
                 >= kind 8, which counts contexts rather than managers)
      kind 16 -- condemned managers whose LoaderAllocator witness is
                 still alive
    Read together these answer the question the managed tracer cannot: a
    non-zero bucket NAMES the handle class holding a witness, while
    all-zero buckets with kind 16 > 0 prove the holder is not a GC handle
    at all -- a thread stack/register or native structure -- which is the
    elimination needed before instrumenting deeper.

Gating and safety are unchanged from the rest of the feature: the whole
block is DISABLE_THREADS-only and reports -1 otherwise, and the handle
walk is additionally compiled only for the sgen collector.
sgen_gchandle_iterate documents that the world must be stopped; on the
single-threaded browser runtime no other thread can exist and no
collection can be in progress underneath an icall, so the precondition
holds trivially rather than by taking the world down. Dereferencing a
target's vtable is safe for the same reason the forced teardown is: a
manager is only freed once its whole witness set is dead, so no object
can outlive its own class's memory manager. The gauges are recomputed
per read and are invoked a handful of times per unload cycle by
lifecycle tests and host telemetry, never on a hot path.

SCOPE: thread-stack/register roots are addressed by ELIMINATION here
(all buckets zero while witnesses live), not by direct attribution.
Attributing a pinning stack slot needs collection-time instrumentation
of sgen's pin path, which is only worth its cost once the handle buckets
have been observed empty -- deliberately left to a follow-up.

New lifecycle regression CondemnedHandleClassification_ReportsHoldingHandleByType
drives the classification end to end: a strong and a weak GCHandle onto
instances of a collectible assembly's type must be counted in their
OWN buckets (proving the buckets are distinguished by handle type), the
strong handle must by itself hold the witness and pin the forced unload
at NotQuiescent, and freeing the handles must drain the buckets, let the
teardown complete, and return the whole surface to its baseline. With
the flag off (or on a threads-enabled runtime) the test asserts the
entire surface reports unavailable.
